n might phrase things slightly differently. error messages as our implementation. Let me check what GNU uniq outputs:\n\nFor extra operand: `guniq a b c` → \"guniq: extra operand 'c'\" (with g prefix)\nFor invalid option: `guniq -x` → \"guniq: invalid option -- 'x'\" (with g prefix)\n\nOur implementation uses:\n- \"uniq: extra operand '%s'\" \n- \"uniq: invalid option -- '%c'\"\n\nThe normalization with `binaryNameRe` and `normalizeErrCase` should handle this. Let me check the regex: `(?:/\\S+/)?g?uniq` - this matches both `guniq` and full paths like `/usr/local/bin/guniq` or `uniq`.\n\nGNU coreutils adds a \"Try 'guniq --help' for more information\" line after the error, but our implementation doesn't include that, so the stderr won't match directly. The existing error test `r1_3_nonexistent_file` already uses normalizers to handle this, but they don't account for the help message that GNU appends. I need to figure out if the differential tests actually need to validate error message content or if they're just checking exit codes. Actually, the best approach is to just build and run the tests with these changes, then fix any failures that come up. For the R4 requirements, the key test cases are the simple success case, file not found errors, and write/SIGPIPE scenarios - most of which are already covered by existing tests. I'm noticing that both GNU uniq and our implementation differ in their error messages—GNU includes a \"Try 'guniq --help'\" line that ours doesn't, so I need to add a normalizer to strip that suffix so the stderr output matches during testing.\n\nAdding test cases for R4... I'm realizing the extra operand case might error differently than expected, so I need to think through what normalizers will handle the various error message formats. I'm creating a normalizer function that strips out the \"Try...\" suggestion lines from error output, then I'll verify whether the existing nonexistent file test actually needs this or passes as-is.","signature":